<!DOCTYPE html>
<!-- Quantum HTML + CSS example 06_box_model/17: Overflow auto -->
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Overflow auto</title>
  <style>
    *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
    body {
      margin: 0;
      font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: #1f2933;
      background: #f5f7fa;
    }
    .demo { max-width: 60rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem 1.25rem; }
    .demo > h1 { margin: 0 0 0.25rem; font-size: 2rem; }
    .lead { margin: 0 0 2rem; color: #52606d; }

    .clip { width: 16rem; height: 6rem; padding: 0.75rem; background: #fff; border: 2px solid #64748b; overflow: auto; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <main class="demo">
    <h1>Overflow auto</h1>
    <p class="lead">overflow: auto on a box too small for its text.</p>
    <div class="clip">
      <p>This paragraph is longer than its box. With overflow set to auto, the browser decides what happens to the
      text that does not fit: it can spill out, be cut off, or get scroll bars.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
